INT. LAUNDROMAT - DAWN

Humming machines, a bank of dryers. A flickering neon sign reads OPEN 24 HRS. Rain-streaked windows. A vending machine glows in the corner.

MARTHA OYELARAN-COLE (60s, thick glasses, cardigan) folds sheets with practised precision. At the next table, her granddaughter KAYLA (16, headphones, school hoodie) sorts socks without looking up. Beside them, ARTURO VEGA (40s, delivery driver, wet jacket) feeds quarters into a washer. DESIREE BLANCHARD (30s, nurse in scrubs) dozes in a plastic chair. And the attendant, MR. OKONKWO-PRICE (70s, bowtie, name tag), mops by the door.

Quiet. Only machines.

MARTHA
You'll want the warm cycle for those.

KAYLA
They're socks, Gran.

MARTHA
Warm.

Kayla pulls out an earbud and drops a bundle into the machine. Arturo glances at her, then at Martha.

ARTURO
(to Martha)
I'm sorry, is this machine free?

MARTHA
That one's broken.

ARTURO
It took my quarters.

MARTHA
It does that.

MR. OKONKWO-PRICE
(from the door)
I'll refund you, sir.

ARTURO
Thank you.

The bell above the door jingles. A woman in a black dress and mud-streaked heels enters, a garbage bag of clothing in her arms, mascara tracks on her cheeks. This is SOLANGE DELACROIX (30s, composed but wrecked). Everyone looks at her and then pretends they didn't.

SOLANGE
Is there a free machine?

MARTHA
Number six.

SOLANGE
Thank you.

She crosses to number six. Loads the bag. Pours detergent from a bottle she takes out of her purse. Starts it with a shaking hand. Stands there, watching it fill.

Desiree stirs. Her eyes open on the dress, on the heels.

DESIREE
Rough night?

SOLANGE
Rough morning.

DESIREE
I'm sorry.

SOLANGE
Don't be.

Kayla puts the earbuds in her pocket. Martha folds a pillowcase, then another. She places one on top of Solange's bag, quietly.

MARTHA
For your head.

Solange stares at it. Then at Martha. She sits on the bench beside her, folds her hands, and for the first time looks up, directly at the old woman.

SOLANGE
Thank you.

MARTHA
Everyone ends up in a laundromat eventually.

The machines hum. Somewhere a dryer buzzes, and no one moves to open it.
